#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897" :question/problem "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291840} :answer-field/id #uuid "5003cd86-c816-5b76-8f3e-ad794b0ec3b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1d28b87-b5c4-5756-ad04-9539f3fce14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334354" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{3}}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90f2d0d8-960b-595b-a4c2-8e564e576ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291846} {:answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291847} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb2ac4f7-d05e-5e07-be02-0051da36ac0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb95c88c-49bb-5efb-9ee9-31b82db8dea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334370" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504ba205-c349-541c-b744-4c22e4694fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291850} {:answer/id #uuid "cc917853-2cff-50ea-b159-bcf4beeaf0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291851} {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} {:answer/id #uuid "00023582-f48e-51ee-94c5-189c2664b183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291853} {:answer/id #uuid "9d256e7f-9c96-5c13-beb9-4be28f2c02c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e12088-410d-56ff-8b61-0d283d8401f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8db7c7d-2c36-54ae-a136-cfed51754274" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e05cf006-4e35-5836-9c93-3b8d7516c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291878} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2aa493-2f19-5559-b2b5-b6fc6d11ec3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291879} {:answer/id #uuid "867a39fb-d4e9-5177-b01d-2d8e24a09ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291880} {:answer/id #uuid "4da94543-36fc-50ab-9e04-f6a5248a7c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291881} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291882} :answer-field/id #uuid "f459767b-797d-5350-848a-cf0ecc8e9d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c4ec3cb-2a21-5f14-9f3d-91d3ab95f323" :question/math-academy-id "q-88698" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
